## Detecting Web Attacks – TryHackMe Lab
## Overview

This project is based on the “Detecting Web Attacks” room on TryHackMe.
It focuses on identifying and analyzing web-based attacks using log analysis, network traffic inspection, and Web Application Firewall 
(WAF) concepts.

The objective was to understand how attackers exploit web applications and how defenders can detect and mitigate these threats using 
multiple data sources.

## Objectives

  -Understand common client-side and server-side web attacks
  -Analyze web server logs to identify suspicious behavior
  -Inspect network traffic (HTTP requests) for attack patterns
  -Learn how WAFs detect and block malicious requests
  -Correlate multiple data sources to improve detection accuracy

## Topics Covered

  -Web Application Security Fundamentals
  -Client-side vs Server-side Attacks
  -SQL Injection detection patterns
  -Log Analysis techniques
  -Network Traffic Analysis (HTTP requests)
  -Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)
  -Web Application Firewalls (WAFs)
  -Security rule-based detection
  -Attack correlation techniques

## Tools Used

  -TryHackMe Lab Environment
  -Wireshark (network traffic analysis)
  -Web server logs (Apache/HTTP logs)
  -Browser Developer Tools
  -Security concepts aligned with OWASP methodology

## Techniques Applied

  -Filtering and analyzing HTTP request patterns
  -Identifying malicious User-Agent strings (e.g., automated tools like sqlmap)
  -Detecting SQL Injection payloads in URLs and parameters
  -Log correlation to trace attacker behavior
  -Traffic inspection to identify suspicious request repetition
  -Understanding WAF rule logic and defensive filtering strategies

## Key Learnings

  -Attack detection requires correlation between logs and network traffic, not isolated analysis
  -SQL Injection and automated scanning tools leave identifiable patterns in web requests
  -WAFs act as a first layer of defense by filtering and blocking malicious traffic
  -Effective security analysis involves understanding both attack techniques and defensive mechanisms
  -Security analysts must think in terms of attack chains, not single events

## Outcome

This lab strengthened my practical understanding of:

  -Web attack detection
  -Traffic and log analysis
  -Defensive security mechanisms (WAFs)
  -SOC-style investigative thinking

It provided a strong foundation for real-world blue team and security operations work.

